# Environmental Conventions and Biodiversity Hotspots

> International environment treaties are a staple of SSC and RRB general
> awareness papers: which treaty protects wetlands, which one banned ozone
> depleting gases, which one is about the climate. The questions test three
> things only, the year, the place and the subject, so a well-built table
> earns easy marks. The same papers ask which Indian regions are among the
> world's biodiversity hotspots.

## 1. Landmark Conferences and Early Treaties

| Event or treaty | Year | What to remember |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Stockholm Conference (UN Conference on the Human Environment) | 1972 | First major UN environment meeting; led to **UNEP**; 5 June became World Environment Day |
| World Heritage Convention | 1972 | Protects cultural and natural heritage sites |
| **Ramsar Convention** | 1971 | Wetlands of international importance; signed at Ramsar, Iran |
| **CITES** (Washington Convention) | 1973 | Regulates international trade in endangered wild plants and animals |
| **CMS** (Bonn Convention) | 1979 | Conservation of migratory species of wild animals |
| Brundtland Report, "Our Common Future" | 1987 | Popularised **sustainable development** |
| Earth Summit, Rio de Janeiro | 1992 | Produced UNFCCC, CBD and Agenda 21 |

- Ramsar entered into force in **1975**; India joined in **1982**. The
  first Indian Ramsar sites were **Chilika Lake** (Odisha) and **Keoladeo
  National Park** (Rajasthan).
- India's **Montreux Record** entries (wetlands facing ecological change)
  are **Keoladeo** and **Loktak Lake** (Manipur).
- CITES was adopted in 1973 and came into force in 1975, and India became a
  party in 1976.
- **UNEP** is headquartered at **Nairobi**, Kenya.

## 2. Ozone, Climate and Pollutant Treaties

| Treaty | Year | Subject |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Vienna Convention | 1985 | Framework to protect the ozone layer |
| **Montreal Protocol** | 1987 | Phase-out of ozone depleting substances such as CFCs |
| **Basel Convention** | 1989 | Control of transboundary movement of hazardous wastes |
| **UNFCCC** | 1992 | Framework treaty on climate change |
| **Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD)** | 1992 | Conservation, sustainable use and fair sharing of benefits |
| UN Convention to Combat Desertification (UNCCD) | 1994 | Land degradation and drought |
| **Kyoto Protocol** | 1997 | Binding emission cuts for developed countries |
| Rotterdam Convention | 1998 | Prior informed consent for hazardous chemicals and pesticides |
| Cartagena Protocol | 2000 | Biosafety, covering living modified organisms |
| **Stockholm Convention** | 2001 | Persistent organic pollutants (POPs) |
| Nagoya Protocol | 2010 | Access to genetic resources and benefit sharing |
| **Minamata Convention** | 2013 | Mercury; adopted in 2013, in force from 2017 |
| **Paris Agreement** | 2015 | Keep warming well below 2 degrees C, aim for 1.5 degrees C |
| **Kigali Amendment** | 2016 | Montreal Protocol amendment to cut HFCs |

- The **Kyoto Protocol** was adopted in 1997 and came into force in 2005. The
  **Paris Agreement** was adopted at COP21 in 2015 and came into force in
  2016.
- **Kigali** is an amendment to the Montreal Protocol, not a new treaty, and
  it targets **hydrofluorocarbons**, which do not harm ozone but warm the
  climate.
- India announced a **net-zero target for 2070** at COP26, Glasgow, in 2021.
- Secretariats: UNFCCC and UNCCD in **Bonn**, CBD in **Montreal**, CITES in
  **Geneva**, Ramsar in **Gland** (Switzerland).

[tip]
Memory trick: "Montreal mends the ozone, Kyoto cuts carbon, Basel binds waste,
Ramsar rescues wetlands, CITES curbs trade, Minamata mops up mercury."
[/tip]

## 3. Biodiversity Hotspots

[definition]
A **biodiversity hotspot** is a region with at least **1,500 species of
endemic vascular plants** (more than 0.5 percent of the world total) that has
lost at least **70 percent** of its original natural vegetation. The idea was
proposed by ecologist **Norman Myers in 1988** and is now used by
**Conservation International**.
[/definition]

- The world list stands at **36 hotspots**.
- India has parts of **four**:

| Hotspot | Indian area |
| --- | --- |
| **Himalaya** | Eastern and western Himalaya, shared with Nepal, Bhutan and others |
| **Indo-Burma** | North-eastern India (outside the Himalayan zone), shared with Myanmar and others |
| **Western Ghats and Sri Lanka** | Western Ghats, along the west coast |
| **Sundaland** | Nicobar Islands |

- In India's share of **Sundaland**, the lists name the **Nicobar Islands**.
- The Western Ghats are also a **UNESCO World Heritage** natural site
  (inscribed 2012).

## 4. IUCN and the Red List

- The **IUCN** (International Union for Conservation of Nature) was founded in
  **1948** and publishes the **Red List** of threatened species, from
  headquarters at **Gland**, Switzerland.
- Order of categories from most to least threatened: **Extinct, Extinct in the
  Wild, Critically Endangered, Endangered, Vulnerable, Near Threatened, Least
  Concern**. **Data Deficient** and **Not Evaluated** sit outside this ladder.

## 5. Indian Laws and Environment Days

| Law | Year | Subject |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Wildlife (Protection) Act | 1972 | Protects wild animals, plants, parks and sanctuaries |
| Water (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act | 1974 | Pollution of water |
| Forest (Conservation) Act | 1980 | Limits diversion of forest land |
| Air (Prevention and Control of Pollution) Act | 1981 | Air pollution |
| Environment (Protection) Act | 1986 | Umbrella law, passed after the Bhopal gas tragedy |
| Biological Diversity Act | 2002 | Created the **National Biodiversity Authority** at Chennai |
| National Green Tribunal Act | 2010 | Created the NGT for environment cases |

| Day | Date |
| --- | --- |
| World Wetlands Day | 2 February |
| Earth Day | 22 April |
| International Day for Biological Diversity | 22 May |
| World Environment Day | 5 June |
| World Day to Combat Desertification | 17 June |
| International Day for the Preservation of the Ozone Layer | 16 September |

[example]
Question: "The treaty that deals with hazardous waste crossing borders is the
..." Think of the key noun, waste, and match it to Basel (1989). If the key
noun were mercury, the answer flips to Minamata; for persistent organic
pollutants, Stockholm.
[/example]

[summary]
Ramsar (1971) guards wetlands, CITES (1973) trade in endangered species, CMS
(1979) migratory animals, Montreal (1987) the ozone layer, Basel (1989)
hazardous waste, Kyoto (1997) emissions, Stockholm (2001) POPs, Minamata (2013)
mercury and Paris (2015) the climate goal. The Earth Summit of 1992 gave the
UNFCCC and CBD. Of the world's 36 biodiversity hotspots, India holds parts of
four: the Himalaya, Indo-Burma, the Western Ghats and Sri Lanka, and Sundaland
(the Nicobar Islands). The Environment (Protection) Act dates to 1986 and the
Biological Diversity Act to 2002.
[/summary]
